# Project Overview

This is a desktop WPF application built with .NET 10 using MVVM and layered architecture.

The application is used for organizing sports tournaments:
- team registration
- match scheduling
- draws (randomization)
- group stage management
- playoff bracket generation

Primary focus:
- team sports (football/soccer, hockey, floorball)
- strongest emphasis on football tournaments

---

# Product Vision & Purpose

## Purpose
- Provide a simple tool to organize sports tournaments efficiently
- Replace manual spreadsheets or chaotic workflows
- Allow fast creation of tournaments, matches, and brackets

## Target Users
- organizers of amateur or semi-professional tournaments
- non-technical users
- people who need quick, reliable setup of tournaments

## UX / Vibe

The application should feel:

- Structured and organized (like a tournament bracket)
- Fast and responsive
- Practical and productivity-focused
- Sport-oriented (especially football mindset)

Design should reflect:
- clarity over complexity
- efficiency over visual effects

Avoid:
- overengineered UI
- unnecessary animations
- hidden or “magic” behavior

Prefer:
- explicit flows
- predictable screens
- visible tournament structure (groups, matches, brackets)

---

# Core Domain Concepts

The system revolves around:

- Team
- Match
- Group (group stage / pools)
- Tournament
- Round / Stage (group stage, knockout stage)
- Bracket (playoff tree)

Domain logic should include:
- match creation rules
- scheduling rules
- progression logic (group -> playoff)
- score results and standings

---

# Architecture (Layered)

The solution is structured into layers:

- Presentation (WPF UI)
- Application (use cases)
- Domain (core business logic)
- Infrastructure (data access only json, external services)

## Rules

- Domain MUST NOT reference:
  - WPF or UI types
  - Infrastructure concerns (DB, file system, HTTP)
- Application:
  - uses Domain
  - defines interfaces (ports)
  - does NOT use UI types
- Infrastructure:
  - contains EF Core, file IO, APIs
- Presentation:
  - uses Application services
  - contains Views and ViewModels only

---

# MVVM Rules (WPF)

## Views
- no business logic
- code-behind allowed only for:
  - UI concerns (focus, visual state, animation)
- bind everything through ViewModel

## ViewModels
- expose bindable properties
- expose commands via ICommand
- call Application services (never Infrastructure directly)
- do NOT reference View types

## Domain
- contains business rules
- contains entities and value objects
- no UI logic
- no INotifyPropertyChanged

---

# Commands (Custom ICommand)

We use a custom ICommand implementation.

## Strict rules

- DO NOT use:
  - RelayCommand (Toolkit)
  - DelegateCommand
  - any external MVVM frameworks

## Command definition

- Commands MUST be created once (constructor)
- MUST NOT be instantiated in property getters

Correct pattern:
- public ICommand SaveCommand { get; }
- initialized in constructor

## CanExecute rules

- CanExecute MUST reflect:
  - IsBusy
  - selection state
  - validation state

- When state changes:
  - trigger CanExecute re-evaluation

Preferred approach:
- use internal method (e.g., RaiseCanExecuteChanged())

Alternative:
- use CommandManager.InvalidateRequerySuggested() (UI thread)

## Async rules

- IO operations must be async
- never block UI thread (.Result / .Wait forbidden)
- ViewModel handles errors and state

---

# Dependency Injection

- Composition root is in Presentation (App startup)
- Use constructor injection everywhere
- No service locator

---

# Application Layer

Application layer defines:
- use cases
- orchestration logic

Patterns:
- services (e.g., ITournamentService)
- query/command handlers

Responsibilities:
- coordinate Domain logic
- call repositories (via interfaces)

---

# Infrastructure Layer

Contains:
- database (EF Core or other)
- persistence
- external integration

Rules:
- implements interfaces from Application
- must not leak DB-specific code to other layers

---

# ViewModel UX State

Each ViewModel should expose:

- IsBusy
- ErrorMessage (string or structured error)
- optionally notifications

Behavior:
- commands update IsBusy
- errors must be visible to UI

---

# Tournament-Specific Behavior Rules

When generating code:

- respect tournament flow:
  - teams -> groups -> matches -> standings -> playoffs
- scheduling must be predictable
- bracket logic must be deterministic
- group standings must be consistent (points, goals, etc.)

Prefer:
- clear models over clever abstractions
- explicit structures (Group, Match, Bracket)

---

# WPF-specific Performance

- use ObservableCollection for lists
- prefer OneWay binding when possible
- avoid heavy logic in property setters
- UI thread logic stays in Presentation only

---

# Coding Style

- use nullable reference types
- no magic strings for bindings
- small, readable methods
- prefer explicit names

---

# Decision Rules (IMPORTANT)

When unsure:

- prefer simpler solution
- avoid unnecessary abstractions
- follow existing architecture strictly
- do not introduce frameworks

For UI:
- prefer clarity over visual complexity

For domain:
- correctness over convenience

---

# Output Rules for AI

When generating code:

- always specify layer (Presentation / Application / Domain / Infrastructure)
- do not violate architecture boundaries
- use the custom ICommand pattern
- keep MVVM separation strict
- ensure code aligns with tournament domain

If unsure about placement:
- domain logic → Domain
- orchestration → Application
- IO / DB → Infrastructure
- UI binding → Presentation